      "strategy": "capacity",
      "translation": "Not all commercial uses of indigenous knowledge related to genetic resources constitute biopiracy; some projects achieve mutual benefits. Take the Indjalangji-Dhidhanu tribe in Australia as an example. This tribe collaborated with researchers from the University of Queensland to conduct research based on their indigenous knowledge about three-awned grass (a perennial tufted herb with a wide range of traditional uses). The cooperative research agreement signed by both parties includes benefit-sharing clauses. A spin-off company is currently using cellulose nanofibers extracted from three-awned grass to develop medical gel, and composite materials made from this herb have also been patented. According to the agreement, realized benefit sharing includes providing employment opportunities for First Nations youth and funding support for training and education related to the Australian indigenous community."
